Functional Dysphagia
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Sensation of food sticking/lodging, Complete absence of progressive weight loss, Exacerbation by severe stress/anxiety, Normal upper GI endoscopy and manometry

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-017
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe panic and absolute refusal to swallow saliva or liquids due to the sensation of an obstructing throat mass.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and flatmate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient experiencing severe acute dysphagia and intense panic, leading to an absolute refusal to swallow. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively evaluate airway patency, exclude true mechanical obstruction or food bolus impaction, and prevent acute dehydration or an unnecessary emergency department admission prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "There is a lump of food stuck right here in my throat, I can't even swallow my own spit, I'm going to choke."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with a 4-hour history of the subjective sensation of solid food sticking in the mid-oesophagus following a highly stressful dinner. The patient has completely stopped swallowing saliva out of fear. There is a complete lack of progressive worsening from solid to liquid dysphagia over recent months, and a complete absence of severe, unremitting weight loss or true odynophagia. The patient reports severe anxiety and recent occupational stress. They specifically deny acidic regurgitation or classic GORD symptoms.
Functional Status: Highly agitated, sitting rigidly holding a spit cup.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of self-induced dehydration due to severe health anxiety and fear of choking.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 148/90 | HR: 105 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 66 Kg
General: Acutely distressed, expectorating saliva into a cup, visibly hyperventilating.
Physical Exam: Airway is completely patent; no stridor, hoarseness, or drooling from inability to protect the airway. Oropharynx is clear without visible mass, foreign body, or severe erythema. Neck is supple without lymphadenopathy or palpable mass. Basic cranial nerve functions are intact (CN IX, X, XII). Patient was observed to successfully swallow a sip of water during the exam without coughing or aspiration, contradicting the perceived total obstruction. Documented history of entirely normal upper GI endoscopy and formal oesophageal manometry 6 months prior.
Environmental Observations: Dinner plate largely untouched; patient's posture is highly tense.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Functional Dysphagia - Status: Acute severe anxiety flare
Differentials:

True Food Bolus Impaction - Ruled out as the patient was successfully able to swallow water without regurgitation or aspiration during the exam, and the airway is clear.

Oesophageal Malignancy - Ruled out due to absence of progressive weight loss, normal baseline appetite, and recent normal endoscopy.

Achalasia - Ruled out explicitly by the documented normal formal oesophageal manometry showing normal peristalsis.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (due to panic)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours home visit due to an acute panic episode centered on a perceived total oesophageal obstruction. The patient's absolute refusal to swallow, accompanied by tachycardia and hyperventilation, mandated an immediate, hands-on clinical assessment to definitively rule out a true mechanical food bolus impaction or airway compromise. By successfully guiding the patient to swallow a test sip of water and confirming intact neurology and airway patency, the physician rapidly de-escalated the acute psychological crisis. This immediate clinical exclusion prevented an imminent, high-acuity transfer to the emergency department, strictly satisfying the criteria for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Guided oral water challenge performed successfully on-site. Administered a single dose of oral Lorazepam 1mg stat to acutely lower sympathetic tone and severe situational panic.
Positive Behavioral Change: When feeling the sensation of a lump, immediately take a slow, deliberate sip of cold, carbonated water rather than avoiding swallowing. The strong sensory feedback of the carbonation safely descending the oesophagus will consciously override the brain's false 'obstruction' signal, rewarding the patient with restored swallowing confidence.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to speech pathology for reassurance and swallowing mechanics review.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 3 days to address the underlying severe anxiety and occupational stress.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation or call 000 if completely unable to swallow saliva resulting in continuous uncontrolled drooling, development of stridor or wheezing, or coughing up blood.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This diagnosis relies heavily on previous definitive structural imaging; if true regurgitation of undigested food begins, reassessment is needed.
Clinical Course: Functional dysphagia is a sensory processing error where the oesophageal nerves mistakenly report an obstruction due to high muscle tension and anxiety. It is terrifying but structurally completely benign.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call an ambulance immediately if you genuinely cannot swallow your own saliva causing it to spill from your mouth, if your voice changes, or if you struggle to breathe.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your anxiety has caused the muscles around your throat to clamp down tightly, creating the physical sensation of a lump (globus). Because your tests showed a perfectly healthy swallowing tube, the treatment is relaxing the muscles, not performing surgery.
Actionable Steps: Eat meals in a completely distraction-free, quiet environment. Focus entirely on chewing food extensively. Removing the stressful environmental triggers will lower your baseline throat tension, allowing food to pass without the false alarm of sticking.

